<section class="section">
  <div class="container prose" style="max-width:980px">

    <h2 style="margin-top:0">Where GoDaddy is the right call</h2>
    <p>GoDaddy is a good fit at the brand-new experiment stage: the business is unproven, you need everything live this weekend, and the simplest bundle wins. Use GoDaddy, and come back when the business is settled and the leads justify a custom site.</p>

    <h2>Where GoDaddy costs you leads</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Slower pages.</strong> GoDaddy Website Builder sites typically score 40&ndash;65 on Google's speed test for phones, the low end of the major builders. Its heavy code loads for every visitor, so on a phone, more people leave before the page appears.</li>
      <li><strong>Weaker local search.</strong> GoDaddy's business details for Google (schema) are generic and shared across templates. Its city pages all look alike, which Google's map results (the "local pack") increasingly treat as low effort.</li>
      <li><strong>Basic forms.</strong> GoDaddy's generic four-field form doesn't ask about the trade, flag urgent jobs, or fit how you dispatch work.</li>
      <li><strong>A bundle that makes leaving look hard.</strong> With everything in one account, changing websites can seem to mean changing everything. It doesn't: you can keep your domain at GoDaddy and point it at the new site.</li>
    </ul>

